<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?><!--
   Real data extraction rules (ROADMAP Task 15 Phase B), replacing the Android Studio sample
   template that shipped here untouched (every rule commented out) since the project's first
   commit. Governs both of API 31+'s backup channels, cloud-backup and device-transfer, each
   configured DELIBERATELY DIFFERENTLY below, not identically. See backup_rules.xml for the pre-31
   equivalent, which has no such split to make (that file's own comment explains why).

   AGENTS.md §1: "Offline-first, single local SQLite database, zero required external cloud sync or
   accounts." The app's private storage today holds Room's database and the content-addressed
   cover images (`<filesDir>/covers`, AGENTS.md §4); ROADMAP Task 15 Phase B adds a persistent log
   file (`<filesDir>/logs`).

   ### cloud-backup: Google Drive Auto Backup, excluded deliberately, in full
   This is genuinely cloud: bytes leave the device and land on Google's servers, which is exactly
   what AGENTS.md §1's "zero required external cloud sync" rules out. The app already ships its
   own explicit, user-driven alternatives with none of that opacity: `.sqlite` backup/restore and
   CSV export (ROADMAP Task 8), both opt-in, both local until the user themselves picks a
   destination via SAF. Silent, size-capped, not-restorable-on-demand Auto Backup adds nothing
   those don't already cover better, while routing the user's reading library through a cloud
   account this app's premise says it should never need. Every domain the app writes to is
   excluded below, so this channel ends up transferring nothing, matching backup_rules.xml's
   answer for the same question on older API levels.

   ### device-transfer: the covers travel, the database deliberately does not
   Device-transfer (the "Copy your data" cable/Wi-Fi Direct flow during new-device setup) never
   touches Google's servers: Android moves the bytes directly between the two physical devices
   the user is holding. That is a materially different privacy posture from cloud-backup, so
   AGENTS.md §1's "zero required external cloud sync" is not implicated here the way it is above,
   and this section gets its own answer rather than inheriting the blanket exclusion.

   That answer is: cover images yes, database NO. The database's absence is deliberate and is the
   whole point of this section, not an oversight. Device transfer is a raw file copy performed by
   the OS at an instant this app does not choose and cannot checkpoint before. DatabaseBackupUseCase
   already considered and rejected exactly that mechanism for exactly that reason: a WAL-mode
   SQLite database's most recently committed writes can live only in the -wal sidecar until
   SQLite next checkpoints them, so a byte-copy can silently capture a torn or stale snapshot.
   That is precisely why the app's own backup runs VACUUM INTO through the normal pager instead
   (see that class's KDoc at length). Letting Android byte-copy the same database here would
   quietly reintroduce the hazard Task 8 spent that whole design avoiding, with no validation
   step and no user awareness that it happened.

   So the database migrates the way this app already says databases migrate: the user's own
   explicit .sqlite backup/restore (ROADMAP Task 8), which validates a candidate file before
   replacing anything. What device-transfer contributes is the part that genuinely hurts to
   rebuild and is safe to copy: the content-addressed covers. A user who transfers to a new phone
   and then restores their .sqlite backup finds the cover hashes already resolving locally,
   skipping the slow rate-limited re-crawl entirely.

   This section uses "include" only, which (per Android's documented behavior for this schema)
   makes it an ALLOWLIST: the database domain, the log directory, and sharedpref are all excluded
   by omission, with no exclude line needed for any of them. That is the same fail-closed posture
   backup_rules.xml reaches by excluding whole domains outright, expressed here as "list only what
   should survive" because this section needs an allowlist shape anyway.

   The log file is excluded under EVERY path in this project regardless of the cloud-backup vs.
   device-transfer distinction above: it is diagnostic output, not user data a migration is
   meant to preserve, and ROADMAP Task 15 Phase B calls this out explicitly ("Must be excluded
   from backup and export, the non-obvious hazard").
-->
<data-extraction-rules>
    <cloud-backup>
        <!-- Same two domains, same reasoning as backup_rules.xml's pre-31 rules; see that
             file's per-rule comments for the detail (database directory contents, filesDir
             contents including the future logs/ directory, and the defensive sharedpref
             exclusion even though nothing writes there today). -->
        <!-- "root" is a separate domain from the three below, covering files written directly into
             the data directory rather than into one of its known subdirectories; without it the
             exclusion would have a gap. See backup_rules.xml for the full per-domain reasoning. -->
        <exclude domain="root" path="." />
        <exclude domain="database" path="." />
        <exclude domain="file" path="." />
        <exclude domain="sharedpref" path="." />
    </cloud-backup>
    <device-transfer>
        <!-- Content-addressed cover images (AGENTS.md §4), and deliberately nothing else.
             Covers are immutable, content-addressed files: their name IS the SHA-256 of their
             bytes, so a raw file copy is either byte-identical or detectably wrong, and there is
             no consistency window to reason about. They are also the expensive half to rebuild.
             Re-acquiring them means re-crawling Open Library through OpenLibraryCoverRateLimiter's
             quota (100 requests per IP per 5 minutes, see that class), which for a large library
             is a long, rate-limited grind; the database itself, by contrast, restores in seconds
             from a file the user already has. -->
        <include domain="file" path="covers/" />
    </device-transfer>
</data-extraction-rules>
